♥ 9♣ (King-high offsuit)\n- **Stage**: Pre-flop, no public cards yet\n- **Valid Actions**: Fold (-1), Check (0), Raise (≥200), All-in (-2)\n\n## Hand Evaluation\n\n**King-9 offsuit** is a mediocre hand:\n- **Strengths**: King is a high card, potential for high pair or straight\n- **Weaknesses**: Offsuit (no flush potential), 9 is a weak kicker, vulnerable to better Kings (K-A, K-Q, K-J, K-10)\n- **Hand Strength**: Below average for heads-up play (~40-45% equity against random hands)\n\n## Strategic Considerations\n\n### Promising Moves:\n\n1. **Check (0)**: \n   - Low risk, see the flop cheaply\n   - Preserve chips while in the lead\n   - Can fold if flop is unfavorable\n   - Conservative but reasonable given moderate hand\n\n2. **Raise 200 (minimum)**:\n   - Apply pressure, test opponent's strength\n   - Build pot if we have best hand\n   - Risk: 200 chips for information\n   - Could fold out weaker hands or get called by better ones\n\n3. **Fold (-1)**:\n   - Too conservative for heads-up play\n   - K-9 has playable equity\n   - Giving up without seeing flop wastes potential\n\n4. **All-in (-2)**:\n   - Extremely aggressive for K-9 offsuit\n   - High variance, unnecessary risk\n   - Only justified with premium hands pre-flop\n\n## Evaluation\nK-9 offsuit is marginal but playable in heads-up. With a comfortable chip lead and halfway through the match, I should avoid unnecessary risks while staying competitive. Checking allows me to see the flop without committing chips, giving me more information to make better decisions on later streets.\n\n## Reason\nI choose to **Check** because: (1) K-9 offsuit has moderate equity but is vulnerable to domination, (2) I'm currently leading and should avoid high-variance plays, (3) Checking preserves chips while allowing me to see if the flop improves my hand, (4) I can reassess on the flop with more information at minimal cost.\n\n**Answer: 0**"}
